  {
    "knowledge_point": {
      "db_id": 17592186129154,
      "id": "a66188f6-3228-59e9-9766-0f9bacbe8443",
      "title": "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Graph of a Given Polynomial"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "40178f34-5280-5fcf-b4d9-f8dfce9d4e64",
      ":question/problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/generate/scratch/17592186129154/plots/ee2440b96519cb15d9139c647cbe64cc4ff697d5fe329d271131af45f87c00df.png)\n\nThe graph of a polynomial $p(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $x=-3$ and $x=0$ are simple roots.\n- **II.** $x=-2$ is a root of even multiplicity.\n- **III.** $p(x)\\to -\\infty$ as $x\\to -\\infty.$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. The root $x=-3$ is a simple root because the graph crosses the $x$-axis at the point and the curve is not tangent to the $x$-axis at that point. The root $x=0$ has an odd multiplicity because the graph passes through the $x$-axis at this point. The curve is tangent to the $x$-axis at that point, so the root is not simple.\n\n- Statement II is true. The root $x=-2$ has an even multiplicity because at $x=-2$ the graph just touches the $x$-axis and reverses direction.\n\n- Statement III is true. As $x$ moves left in the negative direction towards $-\\infty,$ the $y$ value moves down in the negative direction towards $-\\infty.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only\".",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "8a5165ae-b160-5313-a7b9-274e1f82766c",
          ":answer-field/key": "I",
