    "question": {
      ":question/id": "478a9496-ad10-5240-86e3-9d2eff4f5f1b",
      ":question/problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/generate/scratch/17592186129154/plots/a9aff6d4b22cab6420da728b64d03046c81866fbb7ea869365e991404e6d641c.png)\n\nThe graph of a polynomial $p(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $x=-4$ and $x=3$ are simple roots.\n- **II.** $x=-3$ is a root of even multiplicity.\n- **III.** $p(x)\\to -\\infty$ as $x\\to -\\infty.$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. The root $x=-4$ is a simple root because the graph crosses the $x$-axis at the point and the curve is not tangent to the $x$-axis at that point. The root $x=3$ has an odd multiplicity because the graph passes through the $x$-axis at this point. The curve is tangent to the $x$-axis at that point, so the root is not simple.\n\n- Statement II is true. The root $x=-3$ has an even multiplicity because at $x=-3$ the graph just touches the $x$-axis and reverses direction.\n\n- Statement III is true. As $x$ moves left in the negative direction towards $-\\infty,$ the $y$ value moves down in the negative direction towards $-\\infty.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only\".",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "a10b8b3d-46aa-509a-8d3b-2957e2abd839",
          ":answer-field/key": "I",
